Hyper.catalog("biology", [
  ["biology","Hyper Biology","The science of life from molecules to the biosphere: how cells work and divide, how genes are read, inherited and edited, how evolution shapes every species, and how living things fit together in ecosystems.",""],
  ["chemistry-of-life","The Chemistry of Life","Water, pH and the four families of biological molecules — carbohydrates, lipids, proteins and nucleic acids — and the enzymes that make chemistry fast enough for life, with the energy currency ATP.",""],
  ["biomolecules","Molecules of life","Water, pH and buffers, carbohydrates, lipids, amino acids, protein structure and nucleic acids.","The Chemistry of Life"],
  ["water-in-life","Water and life","Water is small, bent and polar, so its molecules cling together by hydrogen bonds. That one fact explains why bodies and oceans resist temperature change, why sweat cools, why sap can rise, why ice floats, and why oily m","The Chemistry of Life",["chemistry:hydrogen-bonding","chemistry:bond-polarity","physics:specific-heat","physics:latent-heat","physics:surface-tension","chemistry:intermolecular-forces","medicine:body-fluids"]],
  ["ph-in-biology","pH and buffers in living things","The charges on proteins, enzymes and membranes depend on pH, so living things hold it steady with buffers. Blood stays between pH 7.35 and 7.45 thanks to the bicarbonate buffer, whose carbon dioxide the lungs can breathe","The Chemistry of Life",["chemistry:ph-scale","chemistry:buffers","chemistry:henderson-hasselbalch","chemistry:weak-acids","medicine:acid-base-balance","medicine:gas-exchange"]],
  ["carbohydrates","Carbohydrates","Sugars and the chains built from them: glucose and its relatives, joined by glycosidic bonds into disaccharides and into polysaccharides — starch and glycogen to store energy, cellulose and chitin to build walls and skel","The Chemistry of Life",["chemistry:carbohydrates","chemistry:functional-groups","chemistry:polymers","chemistry:stereoisomers","medicine:macronutrients","medicine:glucose-regulation"]],
  ["lipids","Lipids","The oily molecules of life: fats and oils that store energy, phospholipids that assemble themselves into membranes, steroids such as cholesterol and its hormones, and waxes. They are grouped together because they do not ","The Chemistry of Life",["chemistry:lipids","chemistry:functional-groups","medicine:cholesterol-lipids","medicine:macronutrients","chemistry:intermolecular-forces"]],
  ["amino-acids","Amino acids and peptide bonds","Every protein is a chain of the same twenty amino acids, each with an amino group, a carboxyl group and one of twenty side chains. Peptide bonds join them head to tail into a chain whose rigid, flat links shape how prote","The Chemistry of Life",["chemistry:amino-acids-proteins","chemistry:functional-groups","chemistry:stereoisomers","chemistry:weak-acids","medicine:macronutrients"]],
  ["protein-structure","Protein structure and folding","A protein chain folds into one precise shape — helices and sheets packed around an oily core — and its function depends on that shape. The sequence holds the information for the fold, but the folded state is only slightl","The Chemistry of Life",["chemistry:intermolecular-forces","chemistry:gibbs-energy","medicine:oxygen-transport"]],
  ["nucleic-acids","Nucleic acids","DNA and RNA are chains of nucleotides — a sugar, a phosphate and a base. DNA's two antiparallel strands pair A with T and G with C in a double helix, so each strand is a template for the other; single-stranded RNA copies","The Chemistry of Life",["chemistry:nucleic-acids","chemistry:hydrogen-bonding","medicine:dna-genes"]],
  ["enzymes-topic","Enzymes and energy","How enzymes work, Michaelis–Menten kinetics, inhibition, regulation, ATP and free energy.","The Chemistry of Life"],
  ["enzymes","How enzymes work","Enzymes are biological catalysts, mostly proteins, that speed up reactions millions of times or more by binding the transition state and lowering the activation energy — without changing the equilibrium. Each works best ","The Chemistry of Life",["chemistry:catalysis","chemistry:arrhenius-equation","chemistry:collision-theory","medicine:vitamins-minerals"]],
  ["enzyme-inhibition","Enzyme inhibition","Inhibitors slow enzymes. Competitive ones fill the active site and raise the apparent Km; non-competitive ones bind elsewhere and lower Vmax; uncompetitive ones bind only the enzyme–substrate complex; irreversible ones d","The Chemistry of Life",["chemistry:equilibrium-constant","medicine:how-drugs-work","medicine:antibiotics","medicine:dose-response","medicine:poisoning-overdose"]],
  ["enzyme-regulation","Allosteric regulation and feedback","Cells tune their enzymes to demand. Allosteric enzymes change shape when effectors bind away from the active site and often respond to substrate with a sharp, sigmoid curve (the Hill equation); end products switch off th","The Chemistry of Life",["medicine:oxygen-transport","medicine:hormone-feedback"]],
  ["atp-energy","ATP and energy coupling","ATP is the energy currency of every cell. Its hydrolysis to ADP and phosphate releases 30.5 kJ/mol under standard conditions and about 50 kJ/mol in a living cell, and enzymes couple that release to uphill reactions, pump","The Chemistry of Life",["chemistry:gibbs-energy","medicine:metabolism-energy"]],
  ["bioenergetics","Free energy in living things","Living things obey thermodynamics: they are open systems that stay far from equilibrium by consuming free energy. ΔG = ΔG′° + RT ln Q decides which way each reaction runs in a cell, and free energy is carried as phosphat","The Chemistry of Life",["chemistry:gibbs-equilibrium","chemistry:reaction-quotient","chemistry:electrode-potentials","chemistry:nernst-equation","physics:second-law-thermodynamics","medicine:membrane-potential"]],
  ["enzyme-kinetics","Enzyme kinetics: Michaelis and Menten","How fast an enzyme works depends on how much substrate it has: in proportion at first, then levelling off at a maximum as every enzyme molecule is kept busy. Two numbers describe it — the maximum rate Vmax and the Michae","The Chemistry of Life",["chemistry:rate-laws","chemistry:catalysis","chemistry:enzyme-kinetics","pharmaceutics:nonlinear-pk","medicine:how-drugs-work"]],
  ["cells","Cells","The unit of life: its size and structure, prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells and their organelles, membranes and transport, signalling, the cell cycle, mitosis, cell death and stem cells.",""],
  ["cell-structure","Cell structure","Cell theory and size, microscopy, prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells, the nucleus, endomembranes, mitochondria, chloroplasts and the cytoskeleton.","Cells"],
  ["cell-theory","Cell theory and the size of cells","All living things are made of cells, the cell is the smallest unit of life, and every cell comes from another cell. Cells stay small because they live through their surface, and surface grows more slowly than volume.","Cells",["math:surface-area","math:scaling-laws","medicine:cell-structure"]],
  ["microscopy","Microscopes and what they reveal","A microscope magnifies, but what it can show is limited by its resolution — about 200 nm for light, set by the wavelength, and well under a nanometre for electrons. Fluorescent labels light up single kinds of molecule in","Cells",["physics:magnification","physics:resolution","physics:optical-instruments","physics:single-slit-diffraction","physics:de-broglie-wavelength"]],
  ["prokaryotic-cells","Prokaryotic cells","Bacteria and archaea: small cells, usually 0.5–5 µm, with no nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles — one circular chromosome in a nucleoid, 70S ribosomes, a cell wall, and often plasmids, a capsule and flagella.","Cells",["medicine:microbes-types","medicine:antibiotics"]],
  ["eukaryotic-cells","Eukaryotic cells","Cells with a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles — those of animals, plants, fungi and protists. Typically 10–100 µm across, they divide their interior into compartments, each with its own chemistry.","Cells",["medicine:cell-structure"]],
  ["nucleus-ribosomes","The nucleus and ribosomes","The nucleus stores two metres of DNA behind a double membrane pierced by pores that control what goes in and out; ribosomes — machines of RNA and protein, 70S in bacteria and 80S in eukaryotes — read messenger RNA and bu","Cells",["medicine:antibiotics"]],
  ["endomembrane","The endomembrane system","The connected membranes of a eukaryotic cell — nuclear envelope, endoplasmic reticulum, Golgi apparatus, lysosomes, endosomes, vesicles and the plasma membrane — along which proteins and lipids are made, modified, sorted","Cells",["medicine:cell-structure"]],
  ["mitochondria-chloroplasts","Mitochondria and chloroplasts","The energy converters of eukaryotic cells: mitochondria make ATP by respiration and chloroplasts capture light in photosynthesis. Both have two membranes, their own DNA and bacterial-type ribosomes — because both descend","Cells"],
  ["cytoskeleton","The cytoskeleton","A network of protein filaments — actin filaments, intermediate filaments and microtubules — that gives a cell its shape, moves it, carries cargo on motor proteins and pulls chromosomes apart. It is constantly built and t","Cells",["medicine:nerve-cells"]],
  ["membranes","Membranes and transport","The fluid mosaic membrane, diffusion and osmosis, water potential, active transport, endocytosis and exocytosis.","Cells"],
  ["membrane-structure","The fluid mosaic membrane","Every cell membrane is a 5 nm double layer of lipids — fluid like oil and self-assembling — with proteins floating in it. The oily core blocks ions and polar molecules, so proteins decide what crosses.","Cells",["chemistry:lipids","chemistry:intermolecular-forces","medicine:membrane-transport","medicine:cholesterol-lipids"]],
  ["diffusion-osmosis","Diffusion and osmosis","Random molecular motion makes substances spread from high to low concentration — fast over micrometres, painfully slow over centimetres. Osmosis is the diffusion of water across a membrane that holds back solutes: cells ","Cells",["chemistry:molarity","chemistry:osmotic-pressure","math:heat-equation","chemistry:effusion-diffusion","medicine:membrane-transport","medicine:electrolytes"]],
  ["water-potential","Water potential","Water potential ψ measures the free energy of water per unit volume, relative to pure water at atmospheric pressure (zero). Water always moves from higher to lower ψ. In a plant cell ψ = ψs + ψp: solutes make it negative","Cells",["physics:pressure","chemistry:osmotic-pressure","physics:hydrostatic-pressure","chemistry:gibbs-energy"]],
  ["active-transport","Active transport and pumps","Moving a substance against its concentration or electrical gradient costs energy. Pumps spend ATP directly — the Na⁺/K⁺ pump moves 3 Na⁺ out and 2 K⁺ in per ATP — and co-transporters spend the gradients the pumps build.","Cells",["chemistry:nernst-equation","medicine:membrane-potential","medicine:action-potential","medicine:digestion-absorption","medicine:how-drugs-work"]],
  ["endocytosis","Endocytosis and exocytosis","Cells move large things across the membrane in bulk, wrapped in vesicles: endocytosis folds the membrane inwards to take material in (phagocytosis, pinocytosis, receptor-mediated uptake through clathrin-coated pits), and","Cells",["medicine:innate-immunity","medicine:synapses","medicine:cholesterol-lipids"]],
  ["cell-life","The life of a cell","Cell signalling, the cell cycle, mitosis, programmed cell death and stem cells.","Cells"],
  ["cell-signalling","Cell signalling","How cells talk to each other: a signal molecule binds a receptor, a relay of proteins and small \"second messengers\" carries the message inside, and each enzyme step multiplies it — so a few hormone molecules can move a h","Cells",["medicine:hormones","medicine:how-drugs-work","medicine:synapses"]],
  ["cell-cycle","The cell cycle and its checkpoints","The ordered sequence by which a cell grows, copies its DNA and divides — G1, S, G2 and M, about a day in a human cell — driven by cyclins and cyclin-dependent kinases and guarded by checkpoints that stop the cycle when D","Cells",["medicine:cancer-biology","medicine:cancer-genetics","medicine:tumour-growth"]],
  ["mitosis","Mitosis","The division of a nucleus into two identical nuclei: condensed chromosomes line up on a spindle of microtubules, their sister chromatids are pulled to opposite poles, and the cell splits in two. Each daughter gets exactl","Cells",["medicine:cancer-treatment"]],
  ["apoptosis","Programmed cell death","Apoptosis is a tidy, genetically programmed suicide: the cell shrinks, cuts up its own proteins and DNA with enzymes called caspases, and is eaten by its neighbours without inflammation. Tens of billions of cells die thi","Cells",["medicine:cancer-biology","medicine:adaptive-immunity","medicine:heart-attack"]],
  ["stem-cells","Stem cells and differentiation","Stem cells can both renew themselves and give rise to specialised cells. Their potency ranges from the totipotent zygote, through pluripotent embryonic and induced (iPS) stem cells, to the multipotent stem cells that mai","Cells",["medicine:blood-composition","medicine:type1-diabetes","medicine:parkinsons"]],
  ["metabolism","Energy and Metabolism","How cells harvest energy from food — glycolysis, the citric acid cycle, oxidative phosphorylation and fermentation — and how plants capture it from light in photosynthesis.",""],
  ["respiration","Cellular respiration","Metabolic pathways, glycolysis, the citric acid cycle, oxidative phosphorylation and fermentation.","Energy and Metabolism"],
  ["metabolism-overview","Metabolic pathways","Metabolism is the network of chemical reactions in a cell, organised into chains of enzyme-catalysed steps. Catabolic pathways break food down and release energy; anabolic pathways build the cell and spend it. ATP carrie","Energy and Metabolism",["chemistry:redox","medicine:metabolism-energy","medicine:macronutrients","chemistry:electrode-potentials"]],
  ["glycolysis","Glycolysis","Glycolysis splits one glucose into two pyruvate in ten enzyme steps in the cytosol. It needs no oxygen, spends 2 ATP and makes 4, for a net gain of 2 ATP and 2 NADH per glucose — a small yield, but fast and universal.","Energy and Metabolism",["medicine:glucose-regulation","medicine:diabetes","chemistry:gibbs-equilibrium"]],
  ["krebs-cycle","The citric acid cycle","In the mitochondrial matrix, pyruvate is converted to acetyl-CoA and the citric acid cycle oxidises the acetyl group completely to CO₂. Per glucose (two turns) it releases 4 CO₂ and makes 6 NADH, 2 FADH₂ and 2 ATP (as GT","Energy and Metabolism",["medicine:metabolism-energy","medicine:vitamins-minerals","chemistry:gibbs-equilibrium"]],
  ["oxidative-phosphorylation","Oxidative phosphorylation and chemiosmosis","The electron transport chain in the inner mitochondrial membrane passes electrons from NADH and FADH₂ to oxygen and uses the energy to pump protons out of the matrix. The protons flow back through ATP synthase, a rotary ","Energy and Metabolism",["chemistry:electrode-potentials","medicine:poisoning-overdose","medicine:thermoregulation","medicine:membrane-potential","chemistry:nernst-equation","chemistry:gibbs-energy"]],
  ["fermentation","Fermentation and anaerobic respiration","Without oxygen, cells regenerate the NAD⁺ that glycolysis needs by passing its electrons to a molecule made from pyruvate — lactate in muscle and yoghurt bacteria, ethanol and CO₂ in yeast. Fermentation yields only the 2","Energy and Metabolism",["medicine:physical-activity","medicine:alcohol","chemistry:redox-reactions"]],
  ["photosynthesis-topic","Photosynthesis","Photosynthesis, the light reactions, the Calvin cycle, C4 and CAM plants, limiting factors.","Energy and Metabolism"],
  ["photosynthesis","Photosynthesis","Photosynthesis uses light to turn carbon dioxide and water into sugar and oxygen. In chloroplasts, the light reactions split water and make ATP and NADPH; the Calvin cycle spends them to fix CO₂. It feeds almost every fo","Energy and Metabolism",["physics:photon","physics:em-spectrum"]],
  ["light-reactions","The light reactions","In the thylakoid membranes, pigments absorb light and two photosystems in series — the Z-scheme — move electrons from water to NADP⁺, releasing O₂ and making NADPH. Protons pumped into the thylakoid drive ATP synthase; c","Energy and Metabolism",["physics:photon","physics:em-spectrum","physics:color-vision","chemistry:electrode-potentials","chemistry:beer-lambert","chemistry:atomic-spectra"]],
  ["calvin-cycle","The Calvin cycle","In the stroma, the enzyme Rubisco fixes CO₂ onto ribulose bisphosphate; the product is reduced to the sugar G3P with ATP and NADPH, and most of it is recycled to regenerate the acceptor. Three CO₂ make one G3P for 9 ATP ","Energy and Metabolism"],
  ["c4-cam","C4 and CAM plants","C4 and CAM plants avoid photorespiration by concentrating CO₂ around Rubisco. C4 plants fix CO₂ into four-carbon acids in mesophyll cells and release it in bundle-sheath cells; CAM plants fix it at night and use it by da","Energy and Metabolism"],
  ["limiting-factors","Limiting factors of photosynthesis","The rate of photosynthesis is set by whichever condition is in shortest supply — light, CO₂ or temperature. A leaf's rate rises with light and then levels off at saturation; raising CO₂ or warming the leaf lifts the plat","Energy and Metabolism",["physics:light-intensity","math:rational-functions"]],
  ["molecular-biology","Molecular Biology","The central dogma: the structure and replication of DNA, transcription, RNA processing, the genetic code and translation — and how genes are switched on and off, mutated, repaired and hijacked by viruses.",""],
  ["dna-to-protein","From DNA to protein","DNA structure, replication, transcription, RNA processing, the genetic code and translation.","Molecular Biology"],
  ["dna-structure","The structure of DNA","DNA is two long chains of nucleotides wound around each other in a right-handed double helix, held together by A–T and G–C base pairs. Because each base has only one partner, either strand fixes the sequence of the other","Molecular Biology",["chemistry:hydrogen-bonding","chemistry:nucleic-acids","medicine:dna-genes"]],
  ["dna-replication","DNA replication","Before a cell divides it copies all its DNA. The two strands separate and each becomes the template for a new partner, so every daughter molecule keeps one old strand — semiconservative replication — with about one mista","Molecular Biology",["chemistry:isotopes","medicine:cancer-biology"]],
  ["transcription","Transcription","Transcription copies the sequence of a gene into RNA. RNA polymerase opens the DNA at a promoter, reads the template strand and builds an RNA with the same sequence as the coding strand (U in place of T), at a few tens o","Molecular Biology",["medicine:dna-genes"]],
  ["rna-processing","RNA processing and splicing","In eukaryotes the first RNA copy of a gene is edited before it leaves the nucleus: a cap goes on the 5′ end, a poly(A) tail on the 3′ end, and the introns are cut out by the spliceosome. Choosing different exons — altern","Molecular Biology"],
  ["genetic-code","The genetic code","The genetic code is the dictionary that turns three-letter words of mRNA — codons — into amino acids. Its 64 codons stand for 20 amino acids and three stop signals; it is redundant, read in a fixed frame without gaps, an","Molecular Biology",["math:combinatorics"]],
  ["translation","Translation","Translation is protein synthesis: a ribosome moves along an mRNA three bases at a time while transfer RNAs bring the matching amino acids, and the ribosome links them into a chain — about 15–20 amino acids a second in ba","Molecular Biology",["medicine:antibiotics"]],
  ["gene-regulation","Controlling genes","The lac operon, eukaryotic gene regulation, epigenetics, non-coding RNAs, mutations and repair, viruses.","Molecular Biology"],
  ["lac-operon","Gene regulation in bacteria: the lac operon","E. coli makes the enzymes for using lactose only when lactose is present and glucose, its preferred sugar, is scarce. A repressor keeps the genes off unless lactose is around; an activator, CAP, switches them fully on on","Molecular Biology"],
  ["eukaryotic-regulation","Gene regulation in eukaryotes","Our cells control genes at many levels — how tightly the DNA is packed, which transcription factors sit on promoters and distant enhancers, how the RNA is processed, how long it lasts and how often it is translated. Comb","Molecular Biology",["medicine:cancer-genetics"]],
  ["epigenetics","Epigenetics","Epigenetic marks — methyl groups on DNA and chemical tags on histones — change how genes are used without changing the DNA sequence, and many are copied when a cell divides. They let a liver cell stay a liver cell; how f","Molecular Biology",["medicine:cancer-genetics"]],
  ["noncoding-rna","Non-coding RNAs","Only a small part of the genome codes for protein, yet much of it is transcribed. Non-coding RNAs do jobs of their own: ribosomal and transfer RNAs build proteins, small RNAs such as microRNAs and siRNAs silence genes by","Molecular Biology"],
  ["mutations","Mutations and DNA repair","A mutation is a change in the DNA sequence. One base swapped for another may do nothing, change an amino acid or create a premature stop; bases added or removed can shift the reading frame. Cells repair almost all damage","Molecular Biology",["medicine:cancer-genetics","medicine:anemia"]],
  ["viruses","Viruses and how they replicate","A virus is a set of genes — DNA or RNA — packed in a protein coat, with no metabolism of its own. It multiplies only inside a host cell by taking over the cell's machinery; some viruses kill the cell at once (lytic), oth","Molecular Biology",["medicine:hiv","medicine:vaccines","medicine:influenza-covid","medicine:microbes-types"]],
  ["genetics","Genetics","How traits are inherited: Mendel's laws, Punnett squares and probability, dominance, blood groups, meiosis, sex linkage, linkage maps, pedigrees, chromosome changes and polygenic traits.",""],
  ["mendelian","Mendelian genetics","Mendel's laws, Punnett squares, dihybrid crosses, dominance, multiple alleles and chi-square tests.","Genetics"],
  ["mendels-laws","Mendel's laws","Gregor Mendel crossed pea plants and counted their offspring. He found that traits are passed on by discrete factors — genes — that come in pairs, separate cleanly into the sex cells (segregation) and, for different gene","Genetics",["math:probability-basics","medicine:dna-genes"]],
  ["punnett-squares","Punnett squares and probability","A Punnett square lists the gametes of two parents along its edges and the offspring they make in its cells; because every cell is equally likely, counting cells gives probabilities. Behind it are two rules — multiply for","Genetics",["math:probability-basics","math:binomial-distribution","math:conditional-probability"]],
  ["dihybrid-crosses","Dihybrid crosses and independent assortment","Following two genes at once, Mendel found the ratio 9 : 3 : 3 : 1: the alleles of one gene go into gametes independently of those of another, so the chances multiply. Genes that interact give modified ratios such as 9 : ","Genetics"],
  ["dominance-types","Incomplete dominance and codominance","Not every heterozygote looks like one of its parents. In incomplete dominance it is intermediate (red × white snapdragons give pink, and the F₂ comes 1 : 2 : 1); in codominance it shows both forms fully (blood group AB, ","Genetics",["medicine:anemia"]],
  ["multiple-alleles","Multiple alleles and blood groups","A gene can have many alleles in a population, though each person carries only two. The ABO blood groups come from three alleles of one gene — Iᴬ and Iᴮ codominant, both dominant to i — giving four blood groups and six ge","Genetics",["medicine:blood-groups","medicine:antibodies","medicine:pregnancy"]],
  ["chi-square-genetics","Testing ratios with chi-square","Real crosses never give exactly 3 : 1 or 9 : 3 : 3 : 1. The chi-square test measures how far the counts are from the expected ones and gives the chance that a deviation that large would arise by luck alone if the hypothe","Genetics",["math:hypothesis-testing","math:probability"]],
  ["chromosomes","Chromosomes and inheritance","Meiosis, sex linkage, linkage and mapping, pedigrees, karyotypes, polygenic traits and human genetic disease.","Genetics"],
  ["meiosis","Meiosis","The two cell divisions that make eggs, sperm and spores: one diploid cell becomes four haploid cells, each with one chromosome of every pair. Homologues pair, cross over and separate in the first division; sister chromat","Genetics"],
  ["sex-linkage","Sex determination and sex linkage","How sex is set — by X and Y chromosomes in mammals, Z and W in birds, chromosome number in bees, incubation temperature in many reptiles — and why genes on the X chromosome show a special pattern: recessive traits are mu","Genetics",["medicine:hemostasis","physics:color-vision"]],
  ["linkage-mapping","Linkage and gene mapping","Genes on the same chromosome tend to be inherited together unless crossing over separates them, and the closer they are, the less often that happens. Counting recombinant offspring gives the recombination frequency; 1 % ","Genetics",["math:logarithms"]],
  ["pedigrees","Pedigree analysis","A pedigree is a family tree drawn to follow one trait. Because people cannot be crossed like peas, geneticists read inheritance from families: who is affected in each generation, in which sex, and from which parent, reve","Genetics",["medicine:cancer-genetics"]],
  ["karyotypes","Chromosomes, karyotypes and nondisjunction","A karyotype is the full set of a cell's chromosomes arranged in pairs: 46 in humans. When chromosomes fail to separate in meiosis (nondisjunction), gametes gain or lose one, giving trisomies such as Down syndrome — whose","Genetics",["medicine:pregnancy","medicine:cancer-genetics","medicine:screening-harms"]],
  ["polygenic-traits","Polygenic traits and heritability","Height, skin colour, yield and blood pressure vary continuously because many genes, each with a small effect, add up — and the environment adds more. Heritability measures how much of the variation in a population goes w","Genetics",["math:normal-distribution","math:descriptive-statistics","math:binomial-distribution"]],
  ["human-genetics","Human genetic disease","Some conditions come from a single gene, others from extra or missing chromosomes, and most common ones from many genes together with the environment. Why harmful alleles persist, how carriers and genetic tests fit in, a","Genetics",["medicine:dna-genes","medicine:screening-harms","medicine:bayes-diagnosis"]],
  ["biotechnology","Biotechnology and Genomics","Reading, copying, cutting and editing DNA: restriction enzymes and cloning, PCR, gel electrophoresis, sequencing, CRISPR, measuring gene expression — and genomes, bioinformatics, GMOs, gene therapy and the ethics of it a",""],
  ["dna-technology","Working with DNA","Restriction enzymes and cloning, PCR, gel electrophoresis, sequencing, CRISPR and measuring gene expression.","Biotechnology and Genomics"],
  ["restriction-cloning","Restriction enzymes and cloning","Bacteria defend themselves with enzymes that cut DNA at short, specific sequences. Biologists borrowed them to cut a gene out of one molecule and paste it into another — a plasmid that a bacterium will then copy for them","Biotechnology and Genomics",["chemistry:nucleic-acids"]],
  ["pcr","The polymerase chain reaction","PCR copies one chosen stretch of DNA over and over, doubling it every cycle, until a few molecules become billions. Three temperatures, two short primers and an enzyme that survives boiling are all it takes.","Biotechnology and Genomics",["math:exponential-growth-decay","medicine:lab-tests"]],
  ["gel-electrophoresis","Gel electrophoresis","DNA is negatively charged, so an electric field drags it through a jelly of tangled fibres. Small fragments thread through faster than large ones, so a mixture sorts itself into bands by size.","Biotechnology and Genomics",["physics:electric-field","chemistry:chromatography","physics:electric-charge"]],
  ["dna-sequencing","DNA sequencing","Reading the order of bases along a DNA molecule: first by making a nested set of fragments that differ by one base, now by watching millions of copies being copied at once. The cost has fallen by a factor of about a mill","Biotechnology and Genomics",["medicine:cancer-genetics"]],
  ["crispr","CRISPR genome editing","A bacterial defence system, repurposed: a short RNA guide leads a cutting enzyme to one matching sequence in a genome, and the cell's own repair machinery turns that cut into an edit.","Biotechnology and Genomics",["medicine:cancer-treatment"]],
  ["gene-expression-tools","Measuring gene expression","Which genes is a cell using, and how hard? Count the messenger RNA — by amplifying one transcript and timing it (qPCR), or by sequencing all of them at once (RNA-seq).","Biotechnology and Genomics",["medicine:cancer-genetics"]],
  ["genomics-topic","Genomics and its uses","Genomes, bioinformatics, GMOs, gene therapy, biosafety and bioethics.","Biotechnology and Genomics"],
  ["genomics","Genomes and genomics","A genome is the whole DNA sequence of an organism. Comparing genomes — their sizes, their gene counts, how much of them does anything — has overturned a surprising number of expectations, starting with how many genes a h","Biotechnology and Genomics",["medicine:cancer-genetics"]],
  ["bioinformatics","Bioinformatics and sequence alignment","Comparing sequences is the workhorse of modern biology. Alignment scores similarity against a model of how sequences change, and tells you whether a resemblance means shared ancestry or nothing at all.","Biotechnology and Genomics",["math:probability-basics","math:combinatorics","math:exponential-growth-decay"]],
  ["gmos","Genetically modified organisms","Crops and animals whose genomes have been deliberately changed — by adding a gene from another species, or now by editing a letter of their own. What they do, what the evidence on safety says, and why the argument is mos","Biotechnology and Genomics"],
  ["gene-therapy","Gene therapy","Treating a disease by changing the genes in a person's cells: adding a working copy, silencing a harmful one, or editing the sequence. After thirty difficult years it works — for a growing handful of conditions, at extra","Biotechnology and Genomics",["medicine:cancer-treatment","medicine:immunotherapy"]],
  ["biosafety-bioethics","Biosafety and bioethics","The rules and the arguments that surround biological research: containment and oversight, consent and privacy, the line between treating a person and altering a lineage, and who benefits from what is discovered.","Biotechnology and Genomics",["medicine:clinical-trials","medicine:evidence-based-medicine"]],
  ["evolution","Evolution","The idea that makes sense of biology: the evidence, natural selection, Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium, drift, gene flow and mutation, sexual selection and coevolution; speciation, phylogenetic trees, molecular clocks and the",""],
  ["evolution-mechanisms","How evolution works","The evidence, natural selection, Hardy–Weinberg, drift, gene flow and mutation, sexual selection and coevolution.","Evolution"],
  ["evidence-evolution","The evidence for evolution","All living things descend, with modification, from common ancestors. Independent lines of evidence agree on it: fossils in the right order, shared anatomy and embryos, the geography of species, the DNA of every organism ","Evolution",["physics:radioactive-decay"]],
  ["natural-selection","Natural selection","When individuals vary, the variation is inherited and some variants leave more offspring than others, the favoured variants become commoner generation after generation. Natural selection is the only known process that bu","Evolution",["medicine:antimicrobial-resistance","medicine:malaria"]],
  ["hardy-weinberg","Hardy–Weinberg equilibrium","In a large, randomly mating population with no selection, mutation or migration, allele frequencies stay constant and the genotypes occur in the proportions p², 2pq and q². It is the null model that tells us when a popul","Evolution",["math:probability","math:hypothesis-testing","medicine:blood-groups"]],
  ["genetic-drift","Genetic drift","Each generation is a random sample of the genes of the one before, so allele frequencies wander by chance — most in small populations. Drift fixes or loses neutral alleles, wears away genetic variation and can overpower ","Evolution",["math:binomial-distribution","math:random-variables"]],
  ["gene-flow-mutation","Gene flow and mutation","Mutation is the ultimate source of new alleles: slow and steady, about 70 new changes in each human newborn. Gene flow — migrants and their genes moving between populations — spreads alleles and keeps populations alike, ","Evolution"],
  ["sexual-selection","Sexual selection","Selection for traits that win mates rather than help survival: ornaments the other sex chooses and weapons rivals fight with. It explains the peacock's train, the stag's antlers and why males and females of many species ","Evolution"],
  ["coevolution","Coevolution","When two species are each a strong selective force on the other, each evolves in response to the other: predators and prey in arms races, parasites and hosts in endless cycles, flowers and pollinators fitted to each othe","Evolution",["medicine:hiv","medicine:influenza-covid"]],
  ["history-of-life","Species and the history of life","Speciation, phylogenetic trees, molecular clocks, the history of life on Earth and human evolution.","Evolution"],
  ["speciation","Speciation","New species form when populations stop exchanging genes — usually after geography separates them (allopatric speciation), sometimes within one area through ecological divergence or, in plants, doubling of the chromosomes","Evolution"],
  ["phylogenetics","Phylogenetic trees","A phylogenetic tree is a hypothesis of who shares a more recent common ancestor with whom. Read it by its branching points, not by the order of the names along the tips: turning a branch around its node changes nothing.","Evolution",["medicine:hiv"]],
  ["molecular-clock","Molecular clocks","Neutral mutations accumulate at a roughly steady rate, so the number of differences between two species' DNA measures the time since they shared an ancestor — once the clock has been calibrated with fossils or dated even","Evolution",["physics:radioactive-decay","medicine:hiv"]],
  ["life-history-earth","The history of life on Earth","Earth is 4.54 billion years old. Life appeared within its first billion years, filled the air with oxygen, stayed microscopic for most of its history, and then, in the last half-billion years, diversified into animals an","Evolution",["physics:radioactive-decay","physics:radiocarbon-dating","chemistry:isotopes"]],
  ["human-evolution","Human evolution","Humans are one twig of the primate tree. Our lineage split from the chimpanzees' about 6–7 million years ago in Africa, walked upright long before brains grew large, and our own species arose in Africa about 300 000 year","Evolution",["medicine:dna-genes"]],
  ["diversity","The Diversity of Life","Classifying life: taxonomy and naming, the three domains, bacteria and archaea, protists and fungi; the diversity of plants, invertebrates and vertebrates, and the microbiomes that live with them.",""],
  ["classification","Classifying life","Classification and naming, the three domains, bacteria and archaea, protists and fungi.","The Diversity of Life"],
  ["taxonomy","Classification and naming","Every species gets a two-part Latin name and a place in nested groups — genus, family, order, class, phylum, kingdom, domain. Modern classification tries to make every group a whole branch of the tree of life.","The Diversity of Life"],
  ["three-domains","The three domains","All cellular life falls into three domains — Bacteria, Archaea and Eukarya — discovered by Carl Woese in 1977 by comparing ribosomal RNA. Archaea look like bacteria but share key machinery with eukaryotes, which probably","The Diversity of Life"],
  ["bacteria-archaea","Bacteria and archaea","The two prokaryotic domains: tiny cells without a nucleus that invented most of life's chemistry. Bacteria have peptidoglycan walls and ester-linked lipids; archaea have neither, and include the record-holders for heat, ","The Diversity of Life",["medicine:microbes-types"]],
  ["protists","Protists","Every eukaryote that is not an animal, a land plant or a fungus: algae that make much of the world's oxygen, amoebae, ciliates, slime moulds and parasites such as the malaria parasite. A convenient word, not a branch of ","The Diversity of Life",["medicine:malaria","medicine:microbes-types"]],
  ["fungi","Fungi","Absorptive eukaryotes built from threads called hyphae, with walls of chitin — closer to animals than to plants. They decompose wood, feed most plants through mycorrhizae, and gave us penicillin, bread and beer.","The Diversity of Life",["medicine:antibiotics","medicine:poisoning-overdose"]],
  ["plants-animals-diversity","Plants and animals","The diversity of plants, invertebrates, vertebrates and microbiomes.","The Diversity of Life"],
  ["plant-diversity","The diversity of plants","Land plants evolved from green algae about 470 million years ago and conquered dry land in steps: mosses and liverworts, then ferns with water-conducting vessels, then gymnosperms with seeds, and finally flowering plants","The Diversity of Life"],
  ["invertebrates","Invertebrates","Animals without a backbone — about 95 % of animal species, from sponges and jellyfish to snails, octopuses, starfish and the million named insects. A useful word, but not a branch of the tree: it leaves out the vertebrat","The Diversity of Life"],
  ["vertebrates","Vertebrates","Animals with a backbone and a skull: about 74 000 species of fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals. Their history is a sequence of inventions — jaws, bony skeletons and lungs, limbs, the amniotic egg, and warm bl","The Diversity of Life"],
  ["microbiome","Microbiomes","The communities of microbes living on and in plants and animals, and their genes. A person carries about 38 trillion bacteria — roughly one per human cell, not ten — mostly in the colon, where they ferment fibre, make vi","The Diversity of Life",["medicine:digestive-system","medicine:gut-microbiome","medicine:antibiotics","medicine:innate-immunity"]],
  ["plants","Plant Biology","How plants are built and move water and sugar without a heart — transpiration, phloem, mineral nutrition — and how they grow, sense, flower and reproduce: hormones, tropisms, pollination, seeds and photoperiodism.",""],
  ["plant-structure","Plant structure and transport","Plant organs and tissues, transpiration, phloem transport, mineral nutrition.","Plant Biology"],
  ["plant-tissues","Plant organs and tissues","A plant is built from three organs — roots, stems and leaves — made of three tissue systems: a protective skin (dermal tissue), plumbing of xylem and phloem (vascular tissue), and the working and packing cells between th","Plant Biology",["physics:viscosity"]],
  ["transpiration","Transpiration and the rise of water","Plants lift water without a pump. Evaporation from the leaves pulls on unbroken columns of water in the xylem, held together by cohesion, all the way down to the roots. The pull follows a gradient of water potential from","Plant Biology",["physics:surface-tension","physics:pressure","physics:hydrostatic-pressure","physics:viscosity","physics:latent-heat","chemistry:hydrogen-bonding","chemistry:vapor-pressure"]],
  ["phloem-transport","Phloem and the movement of sugars","Sugar made in the leaves travels in the phloem to roots, fruits and growing tips at around a metre an hour. The Münch pressure-flow model explains how: loading sugar at a source draws in water by osmosis and builds press","Plant Biology",["physics:viscosity","chemistry:osmotic-pressure","physics:pressure"]],
  ["plant-nutrition","Mineral nutrition and soil","Besides light, water and CO₂, a plant needs about fourteen mineral elements from the soil — nitrogen, phosphorus and potassium above all — and shows telltale symptoms when one runs short. Roots take them up with membrane","Plant Biology",["chemistry:acid-base-theory","medicine:vitamins-minerals"]],
  ["plant-life","Plant growth and reproduction","Plant hormones, tropisms, flowers and pollination, seeds and germination, photoperiodism.","Plant Biology"],
  ["plant-hormones","Plant hormones","Plants coordinate their growth with a handful of small signalling molecules — auxin, gibberellins, cytokinins, ethylene and abscisic acid, joined by brassinosteroids, jasmonates, salicylic acid and strigolactones. Each h","Plant Biology",["medicine:hormones"]],
  ["tropisms","Tropisms and plant movement","A tropism is growth towards or away from a stimulus: shoots bend to light (phototropism), roots grow down and shoots up (gravitropism), tendrils coil round what they touch (thigmotropism). Most tropisms work by growing f","Plant Biology",["physics:viscosity","physics:em-spectrum"]],
  ["flowering-reproduction","Flowers, pollination and fertilisation","A flower is a shoot built for sex: stamens make pollen, carpels hold ovules. Wind or animals carry pollen to a stigma; a pollen tube grows down to an ovule and delivers two sperm cells — one fertilises the egg, the other","Plant Biology",["physics:viscosity","medicine:allergy"]],
  ["seeds-germination","Seeds and germination","A seed is an embryo plant packed with food in a protective coat and dried to a few per cent water so that it can wait — for months, years, occasionally centuries. It germinates when it has water, a suitable temperature a","Plant Biology"],
  ["photoperiodism","Photoperiodism and flowering","Many plants flower at the right season by measuring the length of the night. Short-day plants flower when the night is longer than a critical length, long-day plants when it is shorter; a brief flash of red light in the ","Plant Biology",["physics:em-spectrum","math:trig-functions"]],
  ["animal-physiology","Animal Form and Function","How animals solve the same problems in different ways: homeostasis, temperature, gas exchange, circulation, osmoregulation, nerves and hormones; muscles, immunity, reproduction, development, and how size shapes everythin",""],
  ["animal-systems","Animal systems","Homeostasis, endotherms and ectotherms, gas exchange, circulation, osmoregulation, nervous systems and hormones compared.","Animal Form and Function"],
  ["animal-homeostasis","Homeostasis in animals","Animals keep their internal fluids steady — temperature, salts, water, sugar, oxygen — by negative feedback: a sensor detects a change, a control centre compares it with a set point, and effectors push it back. Each spec","Animal Form and Function",["medicine:homeostasis-feedback","medicine:hormone-feedback","medicine:body-fluids"]],
  ["thermoregulation-animals","Endotherms and ectotherms","Ectotherms such as lizards and fish take their body heat from their surroundings and control it by behaviour; endotherms such as mammals and birds make their own heat by metabolism, at five to ten times the cost. Insulat","Animal Form and Function",["physics:newtons-law-of-cooling","medicine:thermoregulation","medicine:heat-cold","physics:thermal-radiation","physics:latent-heat","chemistry:arrhenius-equation"]],
  ["gas-exchange-animals","Gas exchange across the animal kingdom","Oxygen reaches every animal cell by diffusion, which is fast over micrometres and hopeless over centimetres. Fick's law explains the designs that solve it: skin, countercurrent gills that take 80 % of the oxygen from wat","Animal Form and Function",["chemistry:partial-pressures","medicine:gas-exchange","medicine:oxygen-transport","medicine:ventilation","chemistry:henrys-law","chemistry:effusion-diffusion"]],
  ["circulation-animals","Circulatory systems compared","Beyond a millimetre, animals move a fluid round the body in bulk. Insects and snails bathe their organs in an open circulation at low pressure; annelids, octopuses and vertebrates keep blood in closed vessels. Fish pump ","Animal Form and Function",["physics:hydrostatic-pressure","medicine:heart-anatomy","medicine:cardiac-output","medicine:hemodynamics","medicine:blood-pressure","physics:viscosity"]],
  ["osmoregulation","Osmoregulation and excretion","Keeping the body's water and salts in balance, and getting rid of nitrogen waste: freshwater fish fight a flood of water, marine fish its loss, land animals drought. Nitrogen leaves as ammonia, urea or uric acid dependin","Animal Form and Function",["chemistry:osmotic-pressure","medicine:urine-concentration","medicine:kidney-anatomy","medicine:electrolytes","medicine:body-fluids","chemistry:colligative-properties"]],
  ["animal-nervous-systems","Nervous systems compared","From the diffuse nerve net of a jellyfish to ganglia, brains and spinal cords, all nervous systems use the same neurons, impulses and transmitters. Speed depends on the axon: giant unmyelinated axons in squid reach about","Animal Form and Function",["medicine:action-potential","medicine:neurons","medicine:synapses","medicine:multiple-sclerosis","physics:rc-circuits","physics:capacitance"]],
  ["animal-hormones","Hormones in animals","Hormones are chemical messages carried in the blood to every cell with the right receptor: slower than nerves, but far-reaching and long-lasting. Insects moult and metamorphose under ecdysone and juvenile hormone, tadpol","Animal Form and Function",["medicine:endocrine-system","medicine:hormone-feedback","medicine:thyroid"]],
  ["animal-life","Movement, defence and reproduction","Muscles and movement, immune systems, reproduction, embryonic development, size and scaling.","Animal Form and Function"],
  ["muscles-movement","Muscles and movement","Every animal muscle is myosin pulling on actin: filaments slide, sarcomeres shorten, and force falls as speed rises. Skeletons — hydrostatic, external or internal — turn that pull into motion through levers that trade fo","Animal Form and Function",["physics:torque","physics:static-equilibrium","physics:power","physics:stress-strain","medicine:physical-activity"]],
  ["animal-immunity","Immune systems compared","All animals have innate immunity — barriers, phagocytes, receptors such as Toll that recognise whole classes of microbes, and antimicrobial peptides. Only vertebrates add adaptive immunity, with receptors assembled by sh","Animal Form and Function",["medicine:innate-immunity","medicine:adaptive-immunity","medicine:antibodies","medicine:vaccines"]],
  ["animal-reproduction","Animal reproduction","Animals reproduce asexually — by budding, splitting or unfertilised eggs — or sexually, which costs twice as much but makes varied offspring. Fertilisation is external in water and internal on land, and every species tra","Animal Form and Function",["medicine:reproductive-hormones","medicine:pregnancy"]],
  ["animal-development","Embryonic development","One cell becomes a body by cleavage, gastrulation into three germ layers, and organogenesis. Cells learn where they are from gradients of signals, and Hox genes — lined up on the chromosome in the order of the body parts","Animal Form and Function",["medicine:pregnancy"]],
  ["scaling-allometry","Size, scaling and metabolism","Body size shapes physiology through simple power laws. Surfaces grow as mass to the 2/3, resting metabolism as mass to the 3/4 (Kleiber's law), heart rate falls as mass to the −1/4 and lifespan rises as mass to about the","Animal Form and Function",["math:power-functions","math:logarithmic-scales","math:scaling-laws","math:linear-regression","medicine:metabolism-energy","medicine:energy-balance"]],
  ["ecology","Ecology","Living things and their surroundings: ecosystems, energy flow and food webs, the carbon and nitrogen cycles, biomes; population growth, predators and prey, competition, succession and biodiversity; conservation, climate ",""],
  ["ecosystems","Ecosystems","Ecosystems, energy flow and trophic levels, the carbon and nitrogen cycles, biomes.","Ecology"],
  ["ecosystems-intro","Ecosystems","An ecosystem is a community of living things together with the non-living world they exchange energy and matter with. Energy flows through it once, from sunlight to heat; the atoms of life go round and round.","Ecology",["physics:conservation-of-energy","physics:second-law-thermodynamics"]],
  ["energy-flow","Energy flow and trophic levels","Energy passes up food chains from plants to herbivores to carnivores, and at each step roughly nine-tenths of it is lost as heat. That is why food chains are short, top predators are rare, and pyramids of energy always n","Ecology",["physics:second-law-thermodynamics","medicine:nutrition","physics:entropy"]],
  ["carbon-cycle","The carbon cycle","Carbon moves between the air, living things, soils, the oceans and rocks. The natural flows are huge but balanced; humans now add about 11 GtC a year, of which roughly half stays in the air — raising CO₂ from 280 ppm bef","Ecology",["chemistry:henrys-law","chemistry:ph-scale","chemistry:weak-acids","physics:radiocarbon-dating"]],
  ["nitrogen-cycle","The nitrogen cycle","Nitrogen gas makes up 78 % of the air but its triple bond makes it useless to most life until bacteria — or industry — fix it into ammonia. Microbes then turn ammonium into nitrate and eventually back into nitrogen gas. ","Ecology",["chemistry:redox-reactions","chemistry:catalysis","chemistry:le-chatelier"]],
  ["biomes","Biomes","Biomes are the great vegetation types of the world — rainforest, savanna, desert, grassland, temperate and boreal forest, tundra — each shaped mainly by temperature and rainfall. Unrelated plants in the same climate come","Ecology",["physics:solar-constant"]],
  ["populations","Populations and communities","Population growth, predators and prey, competition and niches, succession, measuring biodiversity.","Ecology"],
  ["population-growth","Population growth","A population with plenty of resources grows exponentially, doubling at a steady interval ln 2/r. As it becomes crowded its growth slows and levels off at the carrying capacity K — the S-shaped logistic curve, which grows","Ecology",["math:exponential-models","math:logistic-equation","medicine:epidemics","finance:compound-interest"]],
  ["predator-prey","Predators and prey","More prey feed more predators, more predators eat down the prey, and the hungry predators then decline: predator and prey populations can cycle, the predator peaks lagging behind. The Lotka–Volterra equations capture the","Ecology",["math:differential-equations-intro","math:eigenvalues","physics:oscillations","chemistry:rate-laws"]],
  ["competition-niches","Competition and niches","Species that need the same limited resources compete. Gause found that two species with identical needs cannot coexist — one excludes the other — so species that do live together divide the resources: different foods, pl","Ecology",["math:systems-of-equations"]],
  ["community-succession","Communities and succession","After a disturbance a community changes in a partly predictable sequence: hardy colonists first, then species that tolerate crowding and shade. Primary succession starts on bare rock or ice, secondary succession where so","Ecology"],
  ["biodiversity","Measuring biodiversity","Biodiversity is the variety of life, from genes to ecosystems. A community's diversity depends on how many species it has (richness) and how evenly the individuals are shared (evenness): the Shannon and Simpson indices c","Ecology",["math:logarithms","physics:entropy","math:power-functions","math:logarithmic-scales"]],
  ["conservation","People and the biosphere","Conservation biology, climate change and living things, invasive species, ecosystem services.","Ecology"],
  ["conservation-biology","Conservation biology","Conservation biology is the science of keeping species and ecosystems alive. Species are now disappearing tens to hundreds of times faster than the fossil background, mainly through habitat loss and overexploitation. Sma","Ecology"],
  ["climate-ecosystems","Climate change and living things","As the world warms, species move poleward and uphill, spring events come earlier and partners can fall out of step, while corals bleach and heat, drought and acidifying seas push others beyond their limits. The speed of ","Ecology",["chemistry:henrys-law","physics:thermal-radiation"]],
  ["invasive-species","Invasive species","People carry species around the world, deliberately and by accident. Most newcomers fail, but a few spread explosively where they have no enemies and harm native life, farming or health — like rabbits and cane toads in A","Ecology",["math:heat-equation","medicine:infection-spread"]],
  ["ecosystem-services","Ecosystem services","Ecosystems do work for people — pollinating crops, cleaning water, storing carbon, building soil, controlling floods and pests — mostly unpaid and unnoticed. Putting values on these services helps them count in decisions","Ecology",["finance:dcf-valuation","finance:compound-interest","medicine:environmental-health"]],
  ["behaviour","Animal Behaviour","Why animals do what they do: innate and learned behaviour, communication, social life and altruism, foraging, migration and navigation, and the clocks inside living things.",""],
  ["behaviour-topic","Behaviour","Innate and learned behaviour, communication, social behaviour and altruism, foraging, migration, biological rhythms.","Animal Behaviour"],
  ["innate-learned","Innate and learned behaviour","Some behaviour appears in every normal member of a species without practice — a gull chick pecking at a red spot, a goose rolling back an egg. Other behaviour is shaped by experience: habituation, conditioning, imprintin","Animal Behaviour",["medicine:neurons","medicine:brain-regions"]],
  ["communication","Animal communication","Signals are acts or structures that evolved to change what other animals do: scents, calls, colours, dances. A honeybee's waggle dance tells nestmates the direction of food as an angle to the sun and its distance as the ","Animal Behaviour",["physics:sound-waves","physics:polarization","medicine:hearing-balance","finance:game-theory"]],
  ["social-behaviour","Social behaviour and altruism","Living in groups brings safety and costs. Animals that sacrifice themselves for others are explained by kin selection — Hamilton's rule rB C — and, between non-relatives, by reciprocity, which game theory analyses as the","Animal Behaviour",["math:probability","finance:game-theory","math:expected-value"]],
  ["foraging","Optimal foraging","Foragers that gain energy faster leave more offspring, so selection should shape which prey they take and how long they stay in a patch. The prey model says rank prey by energy per handling time; the marginal value theor","Animal Behaviour",["math:derivative","math:optimization"]],
  ["migration-navigation","Migration and navigation","Arctic terns fly about 70 000 km a year and godwits cross the Pacific without a stop. Migrants prepare by fattening, steer by the sun (with a clock to allow for its movement), the stars and the Earth's magnetic field, an","Animal Behaviour",["physics:magnetic-field","physics:polarization","math:vector-addition","medicine:vision"]],
  ["biological-rhythms","Biological rhythms","Most organisms keep time with internal clocks. Circadian clocks run with a period close to, but not exactly, 24 hours when left alone and are reset each day by light. A loop of clock genes drives them; in mammals a maste","Animal Behaviour",["medicine:sleep","medicine:endocrine-system","physics:driven-oscillations","math:trig-graphs"]],
  ["microbiology","Microbiology","The invisible majority: bacterial growth, microbial metabolism, antibiotics and the evolution of resistance, biofilms and quorum sensing, microbes in food and industry, and the aseptic technique that keeps cultures pure.",""],
  ["microbes-topic","Microbes","Bacterial growth, microbial metabolism, antibiotics and resistance, biofilms, microbes in industry, aseptic technique.","Microbiology"],
  ["bacterial-growth","Bacterial growth","Bacteria multiply by binary fission, so a population doubles every generation time — twenty minutes for E. coli in rich broth, most of a day for the tuberculosis bacterium. Growth is exponential only while the food lasts","Microbiology",["math:exponential-growth-decay","math:logarithms","math:logistic-equation","medicine:infection-spread"]],
  ["microbial-metabolism","Microbial metabolism","Microbes have found almost every way of making a living that chemistry allows: energy from light or from chemical reactions, electrons from food or from rocks and gases, carbon from sugars or straight from CO₂. Some brea","Microbiology",["chemistry:redox-reactions","chemistry:gibbs-energy"]],
  ["antibiotic-resistance","Antibiotics and resistance","Antibiotics kill bacteria or stop their growth by hitting targets our own cells lack or build differently — the cell wall, the bacterial ribosome, DNA gyrase, folate synthesis. In a population of billions a few resistant","Microbiology",["medicine:antibiotics","medicine:antimicrobial-resistance","medicine:tuberculosis","medicine:how-drugs-work"]],
  ["biofilms","Biofilms and quorum sensing","Most bacteria in nature live not as lone swimmers but in biofilms — crowded communities glued to surfaces by a slime they secrete, from dental plaque to ship hulls. Bacteria also sense how crowded they are through the si","Microbiology",["medicine:gut-microbiome"]],
  ["microbes-industry","Microbes in food and industry","People have used microbes for at least 9000 years to make bread, beer, wine, yoghurt, cheese and soy sauce. Today microbes also make antibiotics, citric acid, amino acids, enzymes, vaccines and human insulin, clean our w","Microbiology",["medicine:type1-diabetes","medicine:vaccines","medicine:gut-microbiome"]],
  ["aseptic-technique","Aseptic technique and sterilisation","Aseptic technique is the set of habits that keeps unwanted microbes out of cultures and keeps cultures where they belong. Sterilisation — by steam under pressure, dry heat, filtration, radiation or chemicals — kills or r","Microbiology",["chemistry:dilution","math:logarithms","physics:radioactive-decay","medicine:infection-spread"]],
  ["biology-methods","How Biology Is Done","Asking good questions of living things: experimental design and controls, statistics for biologists, laboratory calculations and the model organisms that made modern biology.",""],
  ["methods-topic","Methods","Asking questions, experimental design, statistics, laboratory calculations, model organisms.","How Biology Is Done"],
  ["scientific-method-bio","Asking questions in biology","Biology advances by testable hypotheses: predict what should happen if an idea is right, design a test whose result could prove it wrong, and let the data decide — then repeat. Falsification, controls, replication and ho","How Biology Is Done",["math:probability-basics","medicine:evidence-based-medicine","medicine:reading-health-news","math:hypothesis-testing"]],
  ["experimental-design","Experimental design and controls","How to set up an experiment whose answer can be trusted: one variable changed, negative and positive controls, enough independent replicates, random allocation, blocking, blinding, and a sample size chosen for adequate p","How Biology Is Done",["math:probability-basics","math:standard-deviation","medicine:clinical-trials","medicine:evidence-based-medicine","math:normal-distribution"]],
  ["statistics-bio","Statistics for biologists","Living things vary, so every comparison needs statistics: the mean and standard deviation describe a sample, the standard error and confidence interval say how precisely it estimates the population, and tests such as the","How Biology Is Done",["math:standard-deviation","math:normal-distribution","math:hypothesis-testing","math:central-limit-theorem","math:descriptive-statistics","math:linear-regression","medicine:evidence-based-medicine"]],
  ["lab-math","Laboratory calculations","The arithmetic of the bench: moles, molarity and the mass to weigh out, per cent solutions, dilutions with C₁V₁ = C₂V₂, serial dilutions and plate counts, and absorbance with the Beer–Lambert law and standard curves.","How Biology Is Done",["chemistry:mole-concept","chemistry:molarity","chemistry:dilution","chemistry:beer-lambert","chemistry:concentration-units","math:logarithms"]],
  ["model-organisms","Model organisms","A few convenient species — a bacterium, a yeast, a worm, a fly, a weed, a fish and a mouse — taught us most of what we know about genes, cells and development, because life shares its machinery. Their generation times, g","How Biology Is Done"]
]);
